00:05:53.090 --> 00:05:54.190
Now let's look.

00:05:54.190 --> 00:05:59.360
How do we make this application
highly available and scalable?

00:05:59.360 --> 00:06:02.140
First, a good example of
a stateless application

00:06:02.140 --> 00:06:05.350
would be a web front end,
such as an online store.

00:06:05.350 --> 00:06:07.090
You want it to be
highly available,

00:06:07.090 --> 00:06:10.600
so that your users
don't get HTTP 500 when

00:06:10.600 --> 00:06:14.290
checking out while shopping.

00:06:14.290 --> 00:06:15.940
And you want it
to be scalable, so

00:06:15.940 --> 00:06:18.100
that you can get all
the business that

00:06:18.100 --> 00:06:20.350
comes your way on Black Friday.

00:06:20.350 --> 00:06:22.630
And it's worth
noting that stateless

00:06:22.630 --> 00:06:25.570
means that you manage your
machines like a herd of sheep.

00:06:25.570 --> 00:06:27.730
You care about the
size, but you can easily

00:06:27.730 --> 00:06:30.260
replace one machine
with another,

00:06:30.260 --> 00:06:31.920
as they all do the same thing.

00:06:31.920 --> 00:06:36.670
And they don't preserve
any state past VM lifetime.

00:06:36.670 --> 00:06:39.070
So let's go back to the
problems that we have

00:06:39.070 --> 00:06:41.420
discussed in the beginning.

00:06:41.420 --> 00:06:45.740
What to do when your application
may stop responding or crash?

00:06:45.740 --> 00:06:48.910
Managed Instance Groups
offer autohealing for that.

00:06:48.910 --> 00:06:52.450
It promptly detects machines
with failed application,

00:06:52.450 --> 00:06:55.150
and recreates them
for you automatically,

00:06:55.150 --> 00:06:57.370
so that your users
are served again.

00:06:57.370 --> 00:06:59.860
And you don't need to get
a page in such a situation.

00:06:59.860 --> 00:07:02.520
It's all handled.

00:07:02.520 --> 00:07:04.630
Autohealing is
configured very easy.

00:07:04.630 --> 00:07:06.990
All you need to do is to
configure a health check

00:07:06.990 --> 00:07:09.060
for your Managed Instance Group.

00:07:09.060 --> 00:07:10.470
The way the health
check works is

00:07:10.470 --> 00:07:16.360
that it pings each machine on
a specified path and protocol

00:07:16.360 --> 00:07:16.860
and port.

00:07:16.860 --> 00:07:19.080
Let's say myapp http 80.

00:07:19.080 --> 00:07:21.010
It pings it on
regular intervals.

00:07:21.010 --> 00:07:22.770
Let's say every five seconds.

00:07:22.770 --> 00:07:26.280
And it expects an HTTP 200
response within the latency

00:07:26.280 --> 00:07:27.690
that you pre-define.

00:07:27.690 --> 00:07:30.270
And if the response
is not HTTP 200,

00:07:30.270 --> 00:07:33.030
or if it takes longer
than three seconds,

00:07:33.030 --> 00:07:35.980
then the health check
is considered failed.

00:07:35.980 --> 00:07:38.790
And if this health check
fails multiple times,

00:07:38.790 --> 00:07:41.220
the VM is considered
as unhealthy,

00:07:41.220 --> 00:07:43.820
and will be taken
care of or recreated

00:07:43.820 --> 00:07:46.410
by autohealing from
the instance template

00:07:46.410 --> 00:07:49.890
where you have your well-tested
and stable configuration, so

00:07:49.890 --> 00:07:52.740
that it can serve users again.

00:07:52.740 --> 00:07:55.950
It's also worth noting
that health check not just

00:07:55.950 --> 00:08:01.530
works with HTTP protocol, but
also with HTTPS, TCP, and SSL.

00:08:01.530 --> 00:08:07.110
So it can be configured
in a very flexible way.

00:08:07.110 --> 00:08:11.310
Now, Load Balancer also
uses health checking service

00:08:11.310 --> 00:08:14.320
that is configured in
exactly the same way.

00:08:14.320 --> 00:08:17.130
The difference, though, is
that Load Balancer does not

00:08:17.130 --> 00:08:20.160
send new traffic to the virtual
machines that are unhealthy,

00:08:20.160 --> 00:08:23.040
and sends traffic to the
machines that are healthy,

00:08:23.040 --> 00:08:26.280
while original or a
Managed Instance Group

00:08:26.280 --> 00:08:28.140
would recreate a machine
that isn't healthy,

00:08:28.140 --> 00:08:30.380
which is a very different.

00:08:30.380 --> 00:08:32.360
For that reason,
we highly recommend

00:08:32.360 --> 00:08:35.229
using different health checks
for Load Balancer and Managed

00:08:35.229 --> 00:08:36.289
Instance Groups.

00:08:36.289 --> 00:08:38.330
Imagine a situation when
you have a machine that

00:08:38.330 --> 00:08:41.179
is serving at full capacity.

00:08:41.179 --> 00:08:44.000
So it cannot receive
any new connections,

00:08:44.000 --> 00:08:47.030
and would be unhealthy from
a Load Balancer perspective,

00:08:47.030 --> 00:08:49.790
so that Load Balancer needs
to redirect traffic somewhere

00:08:49.790 --> 00:08:50.730
else.

00:08:50.730 --> 00:08:54.440
However, it is still healthy
from the Instance Group

00:08:54.440 --> 00:08:56.600
perspective, because
it is still serving

00:08:56.600 --> 00:08:59.930
while these current
sessions of the application

00:08:59.930 --> 00:09:01.340
is up and running.

00:09:01.340 --> 00:09:04.130
And if you configure a more
aggressive health check

00:09:04.130 --> 00:09:05.540
for Load Balancer--

00:09:05.540 --> 00:09:09.170
let's say you do shorter check
intervals or shorter response

00:09:09.170 --> 00:09:11.670
time outs--

00:09:11.670 --> 00:09:13.880
you will allow Load
Balancer to redirect traffic

00:09:13.880 --> 00:09:15.350
to less busy VMs.

00:09:15.350 --> 00:09:18.200
At the same time,
autohealing will wait a bit

00:09:18.200 --> 00:09:20.330
and will let the VM finish
the current sessions

00:09:20.330 --> 00:09:22.880
and become available
again before it

00:09:22.880 --> 00:09:27.540
makes the final decision
to recreate the VM.

00:09:27.540 --> 00:09:30.990
Another tip for you is make
sure to set an initial delay

00:09:30.990 --> 00:09:33.690
for autohealing to longer than
it takes for your application

00:09:33.690 --> 00:09:35.640
to start serving.

00:09:35.640 --> 00:09:40.110
During the initial period that
starts when VM is created,

00:09:40.110 --> 00:09:42.270
autohealing will not
recreate a machine,

00:09:42.270 --> 00:09:44.580
even if it is unhealthy.

00:09:44.580 --> 00:09:49.080
This is to let your
application time to boot.

00:09:49.080 --> 00:09:51.300
And of course, while it's
starting and initializing,

00:09:51.300 --> 00:09:52.050
it's not serving.

00:09:52.050 --> 00:09:54.660
So it's not going to
pass any health checks.

00:09:54.660 --> 00:09:57.880
And you don't want it to be
recreated by autohealing.

00:09:57.880 --> 00:10:00.750
So if you set the initial
delay to a higher value

00:10:00.750 --> 00:10:02.960
than it takes for your
application to boot,

00:10:02.960 --> 00:10:05.670
autohealing will
only recreate your VM

00:10:05.670 --> 00:10:08.370
if it is unhealthy because
the application has failed,

00:10:08.370 --> 00:10:13.060
and not because it just
hasn't started yet.

00:10:13.060 --> 00:10:14.890
Let's look at how
autohealing works.

