00:12:02.470 --> 00:12:05.840
and the number of consecutive
failures, et cetera.

00:12:05.840 --> 00:12:09.890
So let's go back to
the instance group.

00:12:09.890 --> 00:12:15.380
And as you can see, VM has been
detected that it's failing.

00:12:15.380 --> 00:12:17.930
So it's being
recreated automatically

00:12:17.930 --> 00:12:19.908
by the Managed Instance Group.

00:12:25.070 --> 00:12:26.320
MYKOLA KOMAREVSKYY: All right.

00:12:26.320 --> 00:12:29.350
So now we have a group
of virtual machines

00:12:29.350 --> 00:12:30.250
that are running.

00:12:30.250 --> 00:12:34.990
And if an application fails, it
will be automatically detected,

00:12:34.990 --> 00:12:37.410
and the VM will be
recreated by autohealing.

00:12:37.410 --> 00:12:38.290
Great.

00:12:38.290 --> 00:12:43.240
But what if a bigger problem
happens and the application

00:12:43.240 --> 00:12:45.340
fails in an entire zone?

00:12:45.340 --> 00:12:46.979
In this case,
autohealing won't help

00:12:46.979 --> 00:12:48.520
because the root
cause of the problem

00:12:48.520 --> 00:12:50.740
is likely not on a
single VM, but is

00:12:50.740 --> 00:12:54.290
related to zonal configuration.

00:12:54.290 --> 00:13:00.580
So to protect your application
against such a situation,

00:13:00.580 --> 00:13:02.620
you would use a regional
Managed Instance Group,

00:13:02.620 --> 00:13:05.510
and distribute your machines
across multiple zones,

00:13:05.510 --> 00:13:07.930
so that should a
single zone fail,

00:13:07.930 --> 00:13:11.380
your application can still serve
from the other available zone,

00:13:11.380 --> 00:13:13.450
and the load balancer
will redirect traffic

00:13:13.450 --> 00:13:18.970
to the healthy virtual
machines in those zones.

00:13:18.970 --> 00:13:20.700
You can note here
on this picture,

00:13:20.700 --> 00:13:23.680
that, however, you
will get only 2/3

00:13:23.680 --> 00:13:27.790
of your capacity remaining
in the case of zone failure.

00:13:27.790 --> 00:13:30.340
And if you care about
your high availability

00:13:30.340 --> 00:13:33.730
so much that you want to
have enough machines to serve

00:13:33.730 --> 00:13:38.080
your expected load, even
in case of zonal failure,

00:13:38.080 --> 00:13:42.460
you should make sure that this
2/3 covers that requirements.

00:13:42.460 --> 00:13:46.330
And in this case, you would
need to provision extra VMs.

00:13:46.330 --> 00:13:47.950
Let's take a look
at the example.

00:13:47.950 --> 00:13:52.240
Imagine, at this picture,
you need nine VMs

00:13:52.240 --> 00:13:53.940
to serve your traffic.

00:13:53.940 --> 00:13:57.280
So in order to protect
you against zonal failure

00:13:57.280 --> 00:13:59.110
and still have
sufficient VMs, you

00:13:59.110 --> 00:14:03.610
need to create extra VMs
so that should a zone fail,

00:14:03.610 --> 00:14:06.190
you have, in this
case, 10 VMs available,

00:14:06.190 --> 00:14:08.260
which is more than
nine, and enough

00:14:08.260 --> 00:14:11.250
to cover your expected load.

00:14:11.250 --> 00:14:17.090
In calculating how many VMs
you need to over-provision

00:14:17.090 --> 00:14:20.411
to protect your workload
against zone failure is easy.

00:14:20.411 --> 00:14:22.160
All you need to do is
to divide the number

00:14:22.160 --> 00:14:25.070
of the VMs you need by the
number of the remaining

00:14:25.070 --> 00:14:25.920
available zones.

00:14:25.920 --> 00:14:28.890
So, for example, if
you deploy your group

00:14:28.890 --> 00:14:30.710
to three different
zones, you need

00:14:30.710 --> 00:14:34.610
to add 50% more VMs
that you regularly

00:14:34.610 --> 00:14:36.590
expect from your workload.

00:14:36.590 --> 00:14:39.420
This way, you would protect it.

00:14:39.420 --> 00:14:41.700
Let's see how a regional
Managed Instance Group works.

00:14:47.389 --> 00:14:48.180
CEZARY PIEKACZ: OK.

00:14:48.180 --> 00:14:53.500
So let's go to our regional
Managed Instance Group.

00:14:53.500 --> 00:14:58.860
So as you can see,
we have three zones--

00:14:58.860 --> 00:15:02.310
B, C and F. And in each zone,
we have two instances up

00:15:02.310 --> 00:15:02.850
and running.

00:15:05.850 --> 00:15:08.510
So there is also a load
balancer configured.

00:15:08.510 --> 00:15:13.990
So you have to go to Networking
Services, Load Balancing.

00:15:13.990 --> 00:15:15.110
Load balancer.

00:15:15.110 --> 00:15:17.780
And here is the IP
of the load balancer.

00:15:17.780 --> 00:15:20.690
So we can query
the load balancer

00:15:20.690 --> 00:15:27.890
and see that on each refresh,
a different VM is serving.

00:15:27.890 --> 00:15:30.480
So this application gives
you, as you can see,

00:15:30.480 --> 00:15:35.390
the hostname that the zone that
the VM is in, and the template

00:15:35.390 --> 00:15:37.580
is used that will be
used for the purpose

00:15:37.580 --> 00:15:38.840
of the later presentation.

00:15:38.840 --> 00:15:42.440
So what I've done is, I've
written a simple script that

00:15:42.440 --> 00:15:47.510
will query the load balancer.

00:15:47.510 --> 00:15:52.100
It will query all the VMs
and get the current zone

00:15:52.100 --> 00:15:55.360
that the VM is in, and will
report it on the screen.

00:15:55.360 --> 00:15:57.360
So let's run it.

00:15:57.360 --> 00:16:00.020
So as you can see, all the
VMs are up and running,

00:16:00.020 --> 00:16:02.490
or are serving from all zones.

00:16:02.490 --> 00:16:06.020
So now what we can do is
simulate a zone failure.

00:16:06.020 --> 00:16:08.800
So of course, I will
not bring down the zone

00:16:08.800 --> 00:16:09.800
during the presentation.

00:16:09.800 --> 00:16:18.770
But I can simulate it
by making the VMs report

00:16:18.770 --> 00:16:19.830
unhealthy status.

00:16:19.830 --> 00:16:21.480
So let's do it.

00:16:21.480 --> 00:16:22.225
Regional.

00:16:22.225 --> 00:16:22.725
Disable.

00:16:25.290 --> 00:16:28.010
US-Central1.

00:16:28.010 --> 00:16:33.210
And let's disable
zone F. Disable

00:16:33.210 --> 00:16:37.170
the VMs in zone F.
There is central.

00:16:41.090 --> 00:16:44.840
So now the health check
is configured the same as

00:16:44.840 --> 00:16:46.710
in the previous demo.

00:16:46.710 --> 00:16:51.320
So we need a few seconds
for Load Balancer

00:16:51.320 --> 00:16:54.380
to see that the VMs
stopped responding.

00:16:54.380 --> 00:16:57.090
As you can see, it
happens quite fast.

00:16:57.090 --> 00:17:00.920
So now, our traffic is
served only from zone B

00:17:00.920 --> 00:17:03.600
and zone C. So let's
bring down another zone.

00:17:14.859 --> 00:17:16.515
Let's wait for a
few more seconds.

00:17:20.810 --> 00:17:25.310
And as you can see, only
VMs from zone C are serving.

00:17:25.310 --> 00:17:27.690
So now let's bring
all the zones back.

00:17:39.210 --> 00:17:45.650
So what happens is,
the load balancer

00:17:45.650 --> 00:17:48.770
queries the health
status of the VMs.

00:17:48.770 --> 00:17:52.490
And if the VMs start reporting
that they are healthy again,

00:17:52.490 --> 00:17:56.090
it will redirect traffic
back to those VMs.

00:17:56.090 --> 00:18:01.770
So as you can see, we've
got, already, two zones up

00:18:01.770 --> 00:18:02.270
and running.

00:18:02.270 --> 00:18:04.780
And the third one
will be brought back

00:18:04.780 --> 00:18:05.967
in a matter of seconds.

00:18:05.967 --> 00:18:06.800
Back to you, Mykola.

00:18:10.160 --> 00:18:11.410
MYKOLA KOMAREVSKYY: All right.

00:18:11.410 --> 00:18:14.020
So now we have a
group of VMs where,

00:18:14.020 --> 00:18:15.610
if a single
application fails, it

00:18:15.610 --> 00:18:17.320
gets automatically recreated.

00:18:17.320 --> 00:18:20.530
And it is protected
against zonal failure,

00:18:20.530 --> 00:18:25.245
because the VMs in the
other zones can still serve.

00:18:25.245 --> 00:18:26.420
Back to the problems.

00:18:26.420 --> 00:18:29.980
But what if a load spikes and
lands on insufficient capacity?

00:18:29.980 --> 00:18:32.830
You don't want to lose
business because of that.

00:18:32.830 --> 00:18:35.570
Or, what if a load goes
down, and you have idle VMs?

00:18:35.570 --> 00:18:38.330
You don't want to
pay extra for those.

00:18:38.330 --> 00:18:41.240
Autoscaler of the
Managed Instance Groups

00:18:41.240 --> 00:18:43.910
makes sure that the group
has enough instances

00:18:43.910 --> 00:18:48.590
to serve the load that you
have and adjusts the group

00:18:48.590 --> 00:18:50.910
size in real time.

00:18:50.910 --> 00:18:53.580
Configuring autoscaling
is also fairly simple.

00:18:53.580 --> 00:18:56.760
You turn autoscaling on in
the Managed Instance Group

00:18:56.760 --> 00:18:58.170
configuration.

00:18:58.170 --> 00:19:01.250
Choose the scaling metric
that you want to use.

00:19:01.250 --> 00:19:04.240
CPU usage is a very popular one.

00:19:04.240 --> 00:19:07.090
Select the target
load for that metric--

00:19:07.090 --> 00:19:11.410
for example, 60% CPU utilization
on average per single machine,

00:19:11.410 --> 00:19:15.030
and specify constraints,
so that autoscaler does not

00:19:15.030 --> 00:19:17.460
scale beyond these boundaries,
the minimum and maximum.

00:19:17.460 --> 00:19:20.760
Let's say you want your
group size to be at least one

00:19:20.760 --> 00:19:24.150
VM, but not exceed 10 instances.

00:19:24.150 --> 00:19:27.270
And one more important
parameter is cooldown period.

00:19:27.270 --> 00:19:29.460
This is how long an
autoscaler should

00:19:29.460 --> 00:19:33.230
wait before taking a machine
into account since it

00:19:33.230 --> 00:19:34.560
started booting.

00:19:34.560 --> 00:19:40.280
This is important because
while the VM is booting,

00:19:40.280 --> 00:19:43.880
it might have boot processes
that highly utilize CPU,

00:19:43.880 --> 00:19:47.870
and which is not representative
of the regular serving load,

00:19:47.870 --> 00:19:50.760
and could create a noise
for the autoscaling signal.

00:19:50.760 --> 00:19:53.540
So if you set cooldown
period to longer

00:19:53.540 --> 00:19:56.610
than it takes for your
VM to boot and stabilize,

00:19:56.610 --> 00:19:58.130
you will make sure
that Autoscaler

00:19:58.130 --> 00:20:00.510
will ignore these booting
instances while they're

00:20:00.510 --> 00:20:01.790
loading.

00:20:01.790 --> 00:20:05.280
And without this noise from
the signal, and as a result,

00:20:05.280 --> 00:20:10.460
get more accurate group
sizings by the Autoscaler.

00:20:10.460 --> 00:20:12.830
Let's see how autoscaling works.

00:20:12.830 --> 00:20:16.820
Imagine you have a group
where the load spikes.

00:20:16.820 --> 00:20:18.820
And in the current
number of the VMs,

00:20:18.820 --> 00:20:23.570
the CPU usage goes
significantly above your target,

00:20:23.570 --> 00:20:25.830
which is 60% in this example.

00:20:25.830 --> 00:20:28.190
Autoscaler will
detect the situation

00:20:28.190 --> 00:20:31.640
and add more VMs to bring
the average load back

00:20:31.640 --> 00:20:33.540
to the target value.

00:20:33.540 --> 00:20:38.060
And when, over time, the load
goes down, and becomes lower

00:20:38.060 --> 00:20:41.330
than your target, autoscaler
will also detect the situation

00:20:41.330 --> 00:20:46.670
and will remove the extra VMs,
again, to bring the load back

00:20:46.670 --> 00:20:49.540
to the target.

00:20:49.540 --> 00:20:52.490
A pro tip for you
is, if you want

00:20:52.490 --> 00:20:56.750
to accelerate your scaling, you
should set up a lower target

00:20:56.750 --> 00:20:59.960
value for it to maintain.

00:20:59.960 --> 00:21:02.360
We can look at this
as an example--

00:21:02.360 --> 00:21:04.640
an image, and you have
three VMs, all of which

00:21:04.640 --> 00:21:06.350
are overloaded.

00:21:06.350 --> 00:21:11.010
One is at 80% CPU
utilization, 70%, and 90%.

00:21:11.010 --> 00:21:14.120
And if your target is
60%, the autoscaler

00:21:14.120 --> 00:21:16.130
will resize it to four VMs.

00:21:16.130 --> 00:21:18.850
And if your target is
40%, the autoscaler

00:21:18.850 --> 00:21:20.890
will resize it to six
VMs, which is a faster

00:21:20.890 --> 00:21:22.310
scaling that you could achieve.

00:21:25.170 --> 00:21:28.970
Also, if you use autoscaling
together with regional Managed

00:21:28.970 --> 00:21:32.940
Instance Groups, make sure
that you set a higher maximum

00:21:32.940 --> 00:21:38.720
load than you expect for
your maximum demand--

00:21:38.720 --> 00:21:41.040
a higher maximum number
of instances than you

00:21:41.040 --> 00:21:45.600
expect for a maximum load, so
that Autoscaler has enough room

00:21:45.600 --> 00:21:48.480
to scale up should
a single zone fail.

00:21:48.480 --> 00:21:51.570
This is exactly the same
formula that you would

00:21:51.570 --> 00:21:53.700
use for a non-autoscaled group.

00:21:53.700 --> 00:21:57.270
The only difference is that
for the non-autoscaled group,

00:21:57.270 --> 00:22:02.130
this formula would be applied
to the target number of VMs.

00:22:02.130 --> 00:22:03.870
And these extra VMs
will be actually

00:22:03.870 --> 00:22:06.940
running, whereas in the
autoscaled scenario,

00:22:06.940 --> 00:22:10.680
you would apply this formula
to the maximum boundary

00:22:10.680 --> 00:22:12.000
for the group size.

00:22:12.000 --> 00:22:14.400
And the group will
reach maximum only

00:22:14.400 --> 00:22:18.460
when it is needed
in those zones.

00:22:18.460 --> 00:22:20.280
So let's see how
autoscaling works.

00:22:25.020 --> 00:22:26.390
CEZARY PIEKACZ: Instance Groups.

00:22:26.390 --> 00:22:31.310
So we have an instance
group with only one

00:22:31.310 --> 00:22:35.900
VM that is serving
the web application

00:22:35.900 --> 00:22:37.920
that you've seen before.

00:22:37.920 --> 00:22:43.830
So as I said, this web
application has two functions.

00:22:43.830 --> 00:22:48.010
One is making VMs
report unhealthy status.

00:22:48.010 --> 00:22:52.800
And the second one is generating
the high load on the CPU.

00:22:52.800 --> 00:22:55.040
So what it does
in the background

00:22:55.040 --> 00:22:58.740
is multiplying to run
the numbers in a loop.

00:22:58.740 --> 00:23:02.250
So we'll have high
CPU utilization.

00:23:02.250 --> 00:23:05.760
So let's start the function.

00:23:05.760 --> 00:23:11.090
So in the mean time that we will
wait for the autoscaler to kick

00:23:11.090 --> 00:23:14.190
in, let's look how it's set up.

00:23:14.190 --> 00:23:16.400
So let's open the scaler.

00:23:16.400 --> 00:23:22.640
So we have Autoscaling set
up based on the CPU usage.

00:23:22.640 --> 00:23:25.760
I've set up the target
CPU size to 30%.

00:23:25.760 --> 00:23:30.170
So the autoscaler would spin
up VMs a little bit quicker

00:23:30.170 --> 00:23:33.930
for the purpose of
this presentation.

00:23:33.930 --> 00:23:38.090
I set up the maximum number to
five and the cooldown period

00:23:38.090 --> 00:23:42.210
to 15 seconds, because the
web server is a simple Python

00:23:42.210 --> 00:23:46.750
script, so it takes a
few seconds to start up.

00:23:46.750 --> 00:23:49.490
So let's go back.

00:23:49.490 --> 00:23:52.700
So as you can see,
Autoscaler has already

00:23:52.700 --> 00:23:56.420
detected the high load
on one of the machines,

00:23:56.420 --> 00:24:01.400
and brought up the additional
VMs to compensate for the load.

00:24:06.184 --> 00:24:07.600
MYKOLA KOMAREVSKYY:
So now we have

00:24:07.600 --> 00:24:11.820
a highly available and
autoscaled group of VMs.

00:24:11.820 --> 00:24:14.550
But how do you handle updates?

00:24:14.550 --> 00:24:19.410
How do you rollout
updates in a safe way,

00:24:19.410 --> 00:24:23.490
making sure that your
users don't experience

00:24:23.490 --> 00:24:27.060
downgrade of your service?

00:24:27.060 --> 00:24:31.480
Auto-Updater allows you to roll
out an update in a safe way,

00:24:31.480 --> 00:24:33.480
and in multiple
different scenarios,

00:24:33.480 --> 00:24:36.830
and do it automatically.

00:24:36.830 --> 00:24:40.040
When you do an update, you
want to, at the end of the day,

00:24:40.040 --> 00:24:43.490
get your group from an old
version to a new version.

00:24:43.490 --> 00:24:48.550
And the actual update is all
about the journey from that.

00:24:48.550 --> 00:24:52.060
And you can configure this
and automate this journey

00:24:52.060 --> 00:24:57.740
with the following configuration
options for the Auto-Updater.

00:24:57.740 --> 00:25:00.140
The first one is, you
set up how far would you

00:25:00.140 --> 00:25:01.709
like to roll out the update?

00:25:01.709 --> 00:25:03.500
How many machines would
you like to update?

00:25:03.500 --> 00:25:05.360
You could decide that
you want to update

00:25:05.360 --> 00:25:09.030
half of the machines, or
maybe the entire group.

00:25:09.030 --> 00:25:11.130
The second option
is, how many machines

00:25:11.130 --> 00:25:14.110
do you update at a single time?

00:25:14.110 --> 00:25:15.940
To roll out the
update carefully,

00:25:15.940 --> 00:25:18.870
you don't necessarily
want to update all of them

00:25:18.870 --> 00:25:19.980
at the same time.

00:25:19.980 --> 00:25:23.930
You want to update one or
two, check that they work,

00:25:23.930 --> 00:25:24.930
and then proceed.

00:25:27.560 --> 00:25:30.500
A third option is, how
many extra machines

00:25:30.500 --> 00:25:33.890
do you want to create in the
new versions temporarily?

00:25:33.890 --> 00:25:35.570
This is useful
when you don't want

00:25:35.570 --> 00:25:39.800
to take any of the serving
machines in the old version

00:25:39.800 --> 00:25:41.480
down for an update,
and would rather

00:25:41.480 --> 00:25:43.280
keep them serving as they are.

00:25:43.280 --> 00:25:46.760
Create the machines in the
new version on the side,

00:25:46.760 --> 00:25:49.160
wait until they are
healthy, and start serving,

00:25:49.160 --> 00:25:52.670
and only then remove
the old machines.

00:25:52.670 --> 00:25:55.400
You can also configure
how long an updater should

00:25:55.400 --> 00:25:58.370
wait for the machine in
the new version to boot

00:25:58.370 --> 00:26:03.020
and stabilize, to make sure
that it is truly healthy,

00:26:03.020 --> 00:26:07.470
and only then proceed to
update in the next batch.

00:26:07.470 --> 00:26:09.690
You can also configure
update action.

00:26:09.690 --> 00:26:13.080
By default, it is a recreation
of a VM from a new instance

00:26:13.080 --> 00:26:13.930
template.

00:26:13.930 --> 00:26:16.310
However, you could also
use a rollout strategy

00:26:16.310 --> 00:26:20.340
with the same instance template,
but restarting the VMs instead.

00:26:20.340 --> 00:26:24.120
This is useful when update is
actuated within guest operating

00:26:24.120 --> 00:26:29.160
system agents, such as pop up
agent, for example, on VM boot.

00:26:29.160 --> 00:26:31.950
And finally, you can choose
update mode Proactive, which

00:26:31.950 --> 00:26:34.650
will roll out the
update automatically,

00:26:34.650 --> 00:26:37.620
or Opportunistic, in
which case, the updater

00:26:37.620 --> 00:26:39.610
will only create machines
in the new version

00:26:39.610 --> 00:26:41.110
when new machines
are really needed.

00:26:41.110 --> 00:26:43.890
For example, when the
group is scaled up.

00:26:43.890 --> 00:26:46.650
And it will only remove the
machines in the old versions

00:26:46.650 --> 00:26:49.020
when you need to delete the
machines-- for example, when

00:26:49.020 --> 00:26:50.530
the group should be sized down.

00:26:53.120 --> 00:26:56.270
So important for
you is that if you

00:26:56.270 --> 00:26:59.990
want to update some specific
machines at a specific time

00:26:59.990 --> 00:27:04.250
and do it manually, you could
set up a new instance template

00:27:04.250 --> 00:27:08.090
for your group, set update
mode to opportunistic,

00:27:08.090 --> 00:27:11.060
and then recreate
those specific VMs

00:27:11.060 --> 00:27:14.210
at the time when you want it.

00:27:14.210 --> 00:27:15.740
Updater would
treat your creation

00:27:15.740 --> 00:27:18.260
as an opportunity for
update, and these VM

00:27:18.260 --> 00:27:20.690
will be created from the
new instance template.

00:27:23.590 --> 00:27:25.140
Let's look at what
kind of scenarios

00:27:25.140 --> 00:27:27.420
you could build with
this configuration.

00:27:27.420 --> 00:27:30.335
Rolling update is
a very popular one.

00:27:30.335 --> 00:27:31.710
This is when you
need to roll out

00:27:31.710 --> 00:27:34.920
a patch to your whole group,
or maybe a new feature to all

00:27:34.920 --> 00:27:36.100
of your users.

00:27:36.100 --> 00:27:38.550
So you want the updater
allowed to the entire group.

00:27:38.550 --> 00:27:40.630
New template 100%.

00:27:40.630 --> 00:27:42.610
However, you want to
roll it out safely.

00:27:42.610 --> 00:27:46.440
Let's say you want to update
three machines at a time.

00:27:46.440 --> 00:27:48.390
Wait until they
are up, and if they

00:27:48.390 --> 00:27:51.570
don't pass the health
check, stop the update.

00:27:51.570 --> 00:27:55.530
You can do debugging, or
maybe decide to roll it back.

00:27:55.530 --> 00:27:58.830
And if they do pass
the health check,

00:27:58.830 --> 00:28:01.500
proceed to the next three,
and then the next three,

00:28:01.500 --> 00:28:05.930
and so on, until the
whole group is updated.

00:28:05.930 --> 00:28:09.000
Another useful scenario
is Canary update.

00:28:09.000 --> 00:28:11.830
This is useful when you
want to do AB testing.

00:28:11.830 --> 00:28:13.660
Let's say you have
an online web store,

00:28:13.660 --> 00:28:16.240
and you need to roll
out a feature that

00:28:16.240 --> 00:28:19.280
is expected to increase
your conversion to sales.

00:28:19.280 --> 00:28:23.930
So you would set the new
template to, let's say, 30%

00:28:23.930 --> 00:28:27.830
and wait for a week or a
month until you collect

00:28:27.830 --> 00:28:30.260
enough statistics
to make a decision,

00:28:30.260 --> 00:28:33.200
whether the conversion
has indeed improved,

00:28:33.200 --> 00:28:35.930
and whether you should proceed
with the further rollout,

00:28:35.930 --> 00:28:38.030
or roll it back.

00:28:38.030 --> 00:28:42.260
The beauty of the Canary update
is that the Managed Instance

00:28:42.260 --> 00:28:45.080
Group will keep this ratio
for you as the group scales.

00:28:45.080 --> 00:28:49.890
You don't need to do
anything extra on your own.

00:28:49.890 --> 00:28:51.580
So let's look at
how updater works.

00:28:59.000 --> 00:29:02.470
CEZARY PIEKACZ: So we
have a Managed Instance

00:29:02.470 --> 00:29:04.950
Group that has six VMs.

00:29:04.950 --> 00:29:07.860
All the VMs were created
from the first version

00:29:07.860 --> 00:29:08.770
of our application.

00:29:08.770 --> 00:29:11.880
So we can see that all of them
have instance template number

00:29:11.880 --> 00:29:14.970
one as a source.

00:29:14.970 --> 00:29:19.190
So to make this demo a
little bit more interesting,

00:29:19.190 --> 00:29:21.390
let's use another
application that we've

00:29:21.390 --> 00:29:23.880
written for the purpose
of this presentation.

00:29:23.880 --> 00:29:31.180
So it queries the
VMs via the API,

00:29:31.180 --> 00:29:34.290
and gets the current
status of a VM.

00:29:34.290 --> 00:29:38.010
It can be creating, restarting,
deleting, recreating.

00:29:38.010 --> 00:29:40.920
And it'll also show
you the version

00:29:40.920 --> 00:29:43.830
of the instance template
that is used to create it.

00:29:43.830 --> 00:29:48.080
So as you can see, all
the VMs are in version 1.

00:29:48.080 --> 00:29:51.030
So now let's do
a rolling update.

00:29:51.030 --> 00:29:53.390
Let's do a Canary update
in rolling fashion.

00:29:53.390 --> 00:29:56.120
So that's just that
we want to have

00:29:56.120 --> 00:29:59.310
our second version of our
application to be rolled out

00:29:59.310 --> 00:30:04.280
to 30% of our VMs.

00:30:04.280 --> 00:30:06.590
But we want to do
it in a gradual way.

00:30:06.590 --> 00:30:12.350
So we want to only bring
down one VM at a time,

00:30:12.350 --> 00:30:16.610
and provision an additional
VM at the same time, so we

00:30:16.610 --> 00:30:20.500
would not pay too much
for the additional VMs.

00:30:20.500 --> 00:30:24.150
But at the same time,
we'll keep the pool

00:30:24.150 --> 00:30:27.330
of the VMs in a stable way.

00:30:27.330 --> 00:30:28.610
So let's do the update.

00:30:35.030 --> 00:30:42.290
And as you can see, the first
VM started to be deleted.

00:30:42.290 --> 00:30:45.800
At the same time, the second
VM that we wanted to provision

00:30:45.800 --> 00:30:48.270
has already been created.

00:30:48.270 --> 00:30:51.680
So creating VMs is a little
bit faster than deletion.

00:30:55.000 --> 00:30:56.710
So let's wait for a few seconds.

00:30:56.710 --> 00:31:00.040
It usually takes
about half a minute.

00:31:00.040 --> 00:31:03.970
So as you can see, the first
VM is already up and running

00:31:03.970 --> 00:31:06.100
with version 2 of the template.

00:31:06.100 --> 00:31:10.300
And as soon as the
second VM was created,

00:31:10.300 --> 00:31:12.520
another VM is
being brought down.

00:31:12.520 --> 00:31:15.130
And we are just waiting for
the first VM to be deleted.

00:31:15.130 --> 00:31:19.960
Once the first VM is deleted,
we'll spin up an additional VM,

00:31:19.960 --> 00:31:22.760
and the process continues.

00:31:22.760 --> 00:31:28.760
We can also verify here that
the first VM is all ready.

00:31:28.760 --> 00:31:30.570
Let's refresh.

00:31:30.570 --> 00:31:34.920
The UI console is a little bit
slower than our application.

00:31:34.920 --> 00:31:39.030
So as you can see, this
VM is being deleted.

00:31:39.030 --> 00:31:43.060
We already have two VMs
with instance template 2.

00:31:43.060 --> 00:31:44.170
So here is one.

00:31:44.170 --> 00:31:45.670
Here is the second one.

00:31:45.670 --> 00:31:48.120
And we can also
verify that we have

00:31:48.120 --> 00:31:49.480
two VMs in the new version.

00:31:49.480 --> 00:31:52.990
And the rest of the rest
two are being deleted.

00:32:01.767 --> 00:32:03.850
MYKOLA KOMAREVSKYY: Another
useful update scenario

00:32:03.850 --> 00:32:06.860
is fast rollout without
reduction in service.

00:32:06.860 --> 00:32:10.330
This is useful when
you need to roll out

00:32:10.330 --> 00:32:12.430
an update to your entire group.

00:32:12.430 --> 00:32:16.410
However, you cannot afford
taking down any of the existing

00:32:16.410 --> 00:32:19.150
instances, and would rather
create them on the side.

00:32:19.150 --> 00:32:22.930
So you set the new
template to 100%,

00:32:22.930 --> 00:32:25.410
because you want to roll out
the update to the whole group.

00:32:25.410 --> 00:32:28.090
You set max
unavailable to zero, so

00:32:28.090 --> 00:32:31.780
that it doesn't update any
of the serving old instances.

00:32:31.780 --> 00:32:34.300
And you set max surge to 100%.

00:32:34.300 --> 00:32:36.100
In this case, the
Managed Instance Group

00:32:36.100 --> 00:32:39.880
will create the instances in
the new version on the side,

00:32:39.880 --> 00:32:43.480
wait until they pass the
health check and start serving,

00:32:43.480 --> 00:32:47.320
and only then will
remove the old instances.

00:32:47.320 --> 00:32:49.420
As you can see, with
all of these settings,

00:32:49.420 --> 00:32:54.130
you can build a quite flexible
and automated update scenario

00:32:54.130 --> 00:32:55.420
that works for you.

00:32:55.420 --> 00:32:59.400
It will roll out the
update if the new version

00:32:59.400 --> 00:33:01.660
passes the health check
and is serving well.

00:33:01.660 --> 00:33:06.310
And it will stop the update and
let you intervene if something

00:33:06.310 --> 00:33:08.730
is wrong and the new
instances are not

00:33:08.730 --> 00:33:11.900
passing the health checks.

00:33:11.900 --> 00:33:13.100
Now, it's all good.

00:33:13.100 --> 00:33:15.640
But how does Updater
work with autohealing?

00:33:15.640 --> 00:33:18.650
Could, for example, when
you roll out an update

00:33:18.650 --> 00:33:21.920
and all the instances
fail, could it

00:33:21.920 --> 00:33:24.380
exceed your maximum
available budget?

00:33:24.380 --> 00:33:25.970
The answer is no.

00:33:25.970 --> 00:33:27.340
Let's look at an example.

00:33:27.340 --> 00:33:30.290
Assume you set the max
unavailable to two,

00:33:30.290 --> 00:33:32.710
and updates started rolling out.

00:33:32.710 --> 00:33:34.630
It updated two VMs successfully.

00:33:34.630 --> 00:33:35.780
All good.

00:33:35.780 --> 00:33:40.350
But then an old VM fails
and is being autohealed.

00:33:40.350 --> 00:33:43.210
Updater knows about it, and
reduces its max unavailable

00:33:43.210 --> 00:33:44.930
budget to one.

00:33:44.930 --> 00:33:48.650
So it will only take one
more instance for update.

00:33:48.650 --> 00:33:52.200
And at the end, you will
get one instance updated

00:33:52.200 --> 00:33:54.720
to the new version,
and the old instance

00:33:54.720 --> 00:33:56.300
autohealed and serving.

00:33:56.300 --> 00:34:00.640
So Updater and autohealing
work well together.

00:34:00.640 --> 00:34:02.340
How about Updater
and autoscaling?

00:34:02.340 --> 00:34:05.520
Could update rollout
interfere with the autoscaling

00:34:05.520 --> 00:34:07.420
calculations?

00:34:07.420 --> 00:34:10.570
Let's look at an example.

00:34:10.570 --> 00:34:15.070
Let's say Updater took down
two machines for an update.

00:34:15.070 --> 00:34:17.100
With the constant
load, this would

00:34:17.100 --> 00:34:19.949
lead to a temporary
increase in the average load

00:34:19.949 --> 00:34:22.960
for the remaining
serving machines.

00:34:22.960 --> 00:34:27.465
However, autohealer is aware
about these two VMs that

00:34:27.465 --> 00:34:30.120
are being recreated, and
will take them into account

00:34:30.120 --> 00:34:34.110
when calculating the desired
number of the machines

00:34:34.110 --> 00:34:35.350
for the whole group.

00:34:35.350 --> 00:34:38.790
So it won't provision
any extra machines

00:34:38.790 --> 00:34:41.070
beyond what you need, and
will wait for these machines

00:34:41.070 --> 00:34:42.179
to boot up.

00:34:42.179 --> 00:34:45.719
So Updater and autoscaling
coordinate their work

00:34:45.719 --> 00:34:48.389
and work well together.

00:34:48.389 --> 00:34:51.270
When rolling out an update to
a regional Managed Instance

00:34:51.270 --> 00:34:54.389
Group, Updater will roll
out the new version evenly

00:34:54.389 --> 00:34:57.870
across different zones
to distribute the risk,

00:34:57.870 --> 00:35:01.560
and likewise, when removing
the old virtual machines,

00:35:01.560 --> 00:35:05.880
it will also remove them
evenly across the zones.

00:35:05.880 --> 00:35:08.730
Finally, when your
Managed Instance Group

00:35:08.730 --> 00:35:13.980
is load balanced, and Updater
picks a specific machine

00:35:13.980 --> 00:35:17.250
to update, it will follow
the following process.

00:35:17.250 --> 00:35:21.810
First, this virtual machine
will stop receiving new traffic.

00:35:21.810 --> 00:35:25.530
Then it will wait for the
connection draining timeout

00:35:25.530 --> 00:35:28.170
that is configured
in the load balancer

00:35:28.170 --> 00:35:33.410
to let the current session that
this instance serves finish

00:35:33.410 --> 00:35:35.340
in a graceful way.

00:35:35.340 --> 00:35:40.720
Then it will recreate the
instance from the new template,

00:35:40.720 --> 00:35:44.440
wait until this instance
passes health check,

00:35:44.440 --> 00:35:48.130
and only after that the new
machine will start serving

00:35:48.130 --> 00:35:49.990
and will start
receiving new traffic.

00:35:49.990 --> 00:35:54.240
And Updater will
proceed to the next one.

00:35:54.240 --> 00:35:56.410
So there we have it.

00:35:56.410 --> 00:35:58.950
We have built a scalable
and highly available

00:35:58.950 --> 00:36:01.800
application on top of regional
Managed Instance Group

00:36:01.800 --> 00:36:04.050
that can be load balanced.

00:36:04.050 --> 00:36:06.270
And the group handles
all of the problems

00:36:06.270 --> 00:36:09.240
that we've described above
automatically for you.

00:36:09.240 --> 00:36:13.770
It handles VMs with failed
applications with autohealing.

00:36:13.770 --> 00:36:18.780
It mitigates the
impact of zonal failure

00:36:18.780 --> 00:36:22.320
by distributing your instances
across multiple zones.

00:36:22.320 --> 00:36:24.900
It makes sure that
the size of your group

00:36:24.900 --> 00:36:28.560
is tailored to the variable
load that it might experience.

00:36:28.560 --> 00:36:31.000
And it also allows you
to roll out updates

00:36:31.000 --> 00:36:36.850
successfully, and stop if you
get the unhealthy new version.

00:36:36.850 --> 00:36:39.600
The main thing is that you can
pick any of these automated

00:36:39.600 --> 00:36:44.040
services as you need, one
of them or multiple of them

00:36:44.040 --> 00:36:48.090
or all of them together,
and do the rest if you

00:36:48.090 --> 00:36:49.440
need your custom logic here.

00:36:52.440 --> 00:36:54.300
And in the end,
have peace of mind

00:36:54.300 --> 00:36:58.230
that your application
is well taken care of.

00:36:58.230 --> 00:37:00.965
Now, let's look at how would you
scale a batch pool of workers.

00:37:10.100 --> 00:37:12.760
CEZARY PIEKACZ: So what are
the main characteristics

00:37:12.760 --> 00:37:14.590
of batch workloads?

00:37:14.590 --> 00:37:18.820
So workers are not
needed at all times,

00:37:18.820 --> 00:37:21.680
because you need it only
for the time of computation.

00:37:21.680 --> 00:37:24.250
So you can bring
the workers down

00:37:24.250 --> 00:37:26.980
if you don't have
anything to calculate.

00:37:26.980 --> 00:37:31.820
So you optimize mainly for cost
instead of high availability,

00:37:31.820 --> 00:37:33.550
like in the previous
presentation.

00:37:33.550 --> 00:37:36.040
You also scale
based on the number

00:37:36.040 --> 00:37:39.160
of jobs that are in the queue
instead of, for example, CPU,

00:37:39.160 --> 00:37:41.950
because if you have
computations to do,

00:37:41.950 --> 00:37:44.650
they are usually very
high CPU intensive.

00:37:44.650 --> 00:37:48.490
So scaling based on
the CPU utilization

00:37:48.490 --> 00:37:53.720
would not be as good as scaling
based on the number of jobs.

00:37:53.720 --> 00:37:59.540
So to illustrate how to build
a scalable pool of workers

00:37:59.540 --> 00:38:02.180
for batch processing,
let's look at an example.

00:38:02.180 --> 00:38:07.530
So let's think about
an image stock service.

00:38:07.530 --> 00:38:12.620
So usually how it works is
users upload their photos

00:38:12.620 --> 00:38:14.840
to our service.

00:38:14.840 --> 00:38:19.160
But before putting the
images into the internet,

00:38:19.160 --> 00:38:20.830
we apply the watermark.

00:38:20.830 --> 00:38:26.000
So it's a perfect example
of batch workload.

00:38:26.000 --> 00:38:29.490
So how the architecture
might look like.

00:38:29.490 --> 00:38:32.810
So the user uploads a photo
from a client application.

00:38:32.810 --> 00:38:36.650
For the purpose of the demo that
I will show you in a minute,

00:38:36.650 --> 00:38:39.560
we save it into
the cloud storage.

00:38:39.560 --> 00:38:42.920
When the image is saved, we
also put the name of the image

00:38:42.920 --> 00:38:45.720
into the PubSub task queue.

00:38:45.720 --> 00:38:49.280
And then we create a
Managed Instance Group that

00:38:49.280 --> 00:38:51.510
looks for this PubSub queue.

00:38:51.510 --> 00:38:55.421
It watches for the number
of tasks that are in there.

00:38:55.421 --> 00:38:56.920
And based on the
number of the task,

00:38:56.920 --> 00:39:02.630
it will scale the application,
spin up additional VMs.

00:39:02.630 --> 00:39:06.200
And we have a process
that is running

00:39:06.200 --> 00:39:10.070
on these VMs that takes
the images from the queue,

00:39:10.070 --> 00:39:11.180
the image names.

00:39:11.180 --> 00:39:14.390
Then it downloads the images,
applies the watermark,

00:39:14.390 --> 00:39:19.480
and then uploads them back to
the cloud storage, then output.

00:39:19.480 --> 00:39:22.240
So how it looks like.

00:39:22.240 --> 00:39:25.870
So you have a small number of
users that are putting images

00:39:25.870 --> 00:39:27.020
into the task queue.

00:39:27.020 --> 00:39:29.080
So we have a small
number of VMs.

00:39:29.080 --> 00:39:33.220
But if the traffic grows,
so does the number of VMs.

00:39:33.220 --> 00:39:38.590
But after you process all the
images that are in the PubSub

00:39:38.590 --> 00:39:42.100
queue, autoscaling will
delete all of the VMs,

00:39:42.100 --> 00:39:46.780
so you'll not have to
pay for them highly.

00:39:46.780 --> 00:39:52.540
But if there will be
another pull of tasks

00:39:52.540 --> 00:39:56.680
put into the queue, autoscaling
will pick them up and spin up

00:39:56.680 --> 00:39:58.090
additional VMs.

00:39:58.090 --> 00:40:00.320
So now let's take
a look at the demo.

00:40:07.290 --> 00:40:10.210
So as you can see from the
previous updating demo,

00:40:10.210 --> 00:40:11.730
all the VMs have been updated.

00:40:16.880 --> 00:40:22.610
So first of all, I will show
you the images and the rest

00:40:22.610 --> 00:40:24.170
a little bit later.

00:40:24.170 --> 00:40:31.500
But first of all, let's put
some images into the queue.

00:40:31.500 --> 00:40:40.080
So what this script does
is it pulls all the names

00:40:40.080 --> 00:40:42.780
of the images from the bucket.

00:40:42.780 --> 00:40:46.380
Then it puts them
into the queue,

00:40:46.380 --> 00:40:49.480
so autoscaler could pick it up.

00:40:49.480 --> 00:40:52.110
So let's take a
look at the images.

00:40:52.110 --> 00:40:55.920
So as I said, they are saved
into the cloud storage.

00:40:55.920 --> 00:40:58.825
So let's go there.

00:40:58.825 --> 00:41:00.650
Open our project.

00:41:00.650 --> 00:41:01.930
And we have two folders.

00:41:01.930 --> 00:41:06.820
One is an input that has
images that I've uploaded

00:41:06.820 --> 00:41:08.310
before the presentation.

00:41:08.310 --> 00:41:12.580
They were taken from the recent
trip to the Italian Alps.

00:41:12.580 --> 00:41:16.810
As you can see, the views
were quite spectacular.

00:41:16.810 --> 00:41:23.660
And we have also an output
folder that is empty.

00:41:23.660 --> 00:41:27.243
So let's go back to our
Managed Instance Group.

00:41:32.160 --> 00:41:35.890
And let's see how
it is being set up.

00:41:35.890 --> 00:41:39.100
So we've turned on
autoscaling, as before.

00:41:39.100 --> 00:41:43.620
But this time, instead of
using scaling based on the CPU,

00:41:43.620 --> 00:41:46.860
we've set up scaling based on
the stack driver monitoring

00:41:46.860 --> 00:41:48.220
metric.

00:41:48.220 --> 00:41:50.730
So what we are looking at,
and what is important here,

00:41:50.730 --> 00:41:54.210
is that we are looking at the
number of undelivered messages

00:41:54.210 --> 00:41:54.990
into the queue.

00:41:54.990 --> 00:41:59.540
The rest is basically not
an interest in this now.

00:41:59.540 --> 00:42:03.420
And we have to provide
the subscription name

00:42:03.420 --> 00:42:07.170
so the autoscaler would know
which queue to watch on.

00:42:07.170 --> 00:42:09.570
And also, what is important
that you can specify

00:42:09.570 --> 00:42:12.014
the number of tasks per VM.

00:42:12.014 --> 00:42:12.930
But what does it mean?

00:42:12.930 --> 00:42:15.690
It is that autoscaler will
take the number of tasks

00:42:15.690 --> 00:42:18.750
into the queue, divide
it by the number of tasks

00:42:18.750 --> 00:42:25.387
that each VM can process,
and then spin up the VMs

00:42:25.387 --> 00:42:26.470
to auto process this task.

00:42:26.470 --> 00:42:29.610
So if you have 10 tasks, and
you have two tasks per VM,

00:42:29.610 --> 00:42:32.970
you will have five
VMs in the end.

00:42:32.970 --> 00:42:36.380
So let's go back
to the autoscaling.

00:42:36.380 --> 00:42:36.975
Let's refresh.

00:42:39.700 --> 00:42:43.700
And as you can see, we have
five VMs processing our images.

00:42:43.700 --> 00:42:49.120
So we can go back
to the storage.

00:42:49.120 --> 00:42:52.559
And let's see if there
are any images processed.

00:42:57.440 --> 00:42:57.940
Yep.

00:42:57.940 --> 00:43:00.090
It's already working.

00:43:00.090 --> 00:43:05.660
And as you can see, we apply the
watermark-- the GCP Next logo.

00:43:05.660 --> 00:43:07.740
And everything is
up and running.

00:43:07.740 --> 00:43:09.491
Can we get back to
the slides, please?

00:43:13.330 --> 00:43:17.380
And we have also
another tip for you.

00:43:17.380 --> 00:43:21.520
So you can reduce the
cost of your service using

00:43:21.520 --> 00:43:25.840
the preemptible VMs,
which can be up to 80%

00:43:25.840 --> 00:43:29.530
cheaper than the usual VMs.

00:43:29.530 --> 00:43:30.460
But there is a caveat.

00:43:36.580 --> 00:43:41.920
The VMs last up to 24 hours that
can be preempted at any time.

00:43:41.920 --> 00:43:48.080
But also, autohealing
works with them.

00:43:48.080 --> 00:43:52.570
So if the VMs will
be brought down,

00:43:52.570 --> 00:43:55.570
the autohealing will
watch for the capacity

00:43:55.570 --> 00:43:57.280
of preemptible VMs.

00:43:57.280 --> 00:43:59.920
If the capacity becomes
available again,

00:43:59.920 --> 00:44:02.680
it will bring up the VMs again.

00:44:02.680 --> 00:44:05.140
And also one thing to
note-- that these VMs will

00:44:05.140 --> 00:44:09.430
be brought down gracefully, so
we'll get a 30 second notice.

00:44:09.430 --> 00:44:12.250
So you can run your script,
for example, in a VM,

